<footer><p class="workflow"><b>PAUSE · 2–5 MIN</b> <strong>Predict → Change → Explain</strong><span>What changed? Why? Is heat gained or lost?</span></p><p class="disclaimer">Teaching model, not a clinical prediction. No core temperature or safe exposure time is calculated.</p></footer>
<div class="sr-only" id="live-summary" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"></div>
<dialog id="notes" aria-labelledby="notes-title"><button class="close" id="close-notes" type="button">Close</button><h2 id="notes-title">Inside the model</h2>
<p>V1 separates <b>what the body requires</b> from <b>what the environment permits</b>. The third quantity, actual evaporation, is an idealized assumption. The main screen contains exactly four model controls.</p>
<h3>Fixed assumptions</h3><p>A reference 70 kg adult with 1.8 m² body surface area, skin at 33°C, 0.5 clo light clothing, adequate hydration, standing in air at sea-level pressure. Radiant surroundings equal air temperature; no sun. Body mass is descriptive only: there is no time-dependent temperature model. Real skin temperature changes; fixing it isolates physical exchange.</p><p>Conduction is displayed but approximated as zero because shoe/ground contact is small. Internal conduction through clothing is already included in clothing resistance, and is not a second external heat loss. Respiration, obligatory skin diffusion, sweat-rate limits, wet-clothing effects and movement-generated airflow are omitted. Activity changes heat production only.</p>
<h3>Equations actually used</h3><p>The lecture’s <b>S = M ± C ± R − E</b> groups convection and conduction in C (lecture PDF page 26, slide 29). Internally dry fluxes are positive outward:</p><p><code>S = M − Cconv − K − R − E<br>Ereq = max(0, M − Cconv − K − R)<br>E = min(Ereq, Emax)</code></p><p>If dry losses exceed M, Ereq is zero and S remains negative: evaporation cannot warm the body. Otherwise balance is possible only if capacity meets demand and enough sweat is supplied. This is a feasibility calculation, not a feedback controller or a prediction of sweat secretion.</p>
<p><code>hc = max(3.0, 8.6 v^0.53)<br>Cconv = A fcl hc (Tcl − Ta)<br>R = A fcl fr ε σ [(Tcl + 273.15)^4 − (Ta + 273.15)^4]<br>A (Tskin − Tcl) / Rcl = Cconv + R<br>K = 0</code></p><p>The clothing surface temperature Tcl is solved by bisection between skin and air temperature. Convection and radiation therefore share one clothing resistance; it is not counted twice. The clothing is a uniform effective layer, and radiation uses absolute temperature in kelvin.</p>
<p><code>psat(T) = 0.6108 exp[17.27 T / (T + 237.3)] kPa<br>Δp = psat(33) − (RH / 100) psat(Ta)<br>Re = Rcl / (im LR) + 1 / (fcl LR hc)<br>Emax = max(0, A Δp / Re)</code></p><p>Capacity assumes a fully wet skin surface beneath the fixed clothing resistance. RH is relative to air temperature: even 100% RH can allow evaporation if the air is cooler than skin. When Δp &lt; 0, evaporation is zero in V1; possible condensation heat is omitted and an explicit warning appears. Humidity constrains evaporation, not sweat production.</p>
<h3>Constants, units &amp; provenance</h3><ul>
<li><b>A = 1.8 m²; Tskin = 33°C:</b> fixed curriculum/reference assumptions.</li>
<li><b>Rcl = 0.155 × 0.5 = 0.0775 m² K/W; fcl = 1 + 0.15 × 0.5 = 1.075; im = 0.45.</b> Clothing area and permeability conventions, plus the natural/forced convection coefficient, follow the <a href="https://pythermalcomfort.readthedocs.io/en/stable/_modules/pythermalcomfort/models/two_nodes_gagge.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">documented Gagge implementation</a>. V1 borrows transport relationships, not its physiological controller. hc has units W/(m² K); v is m/s. The 3.0 floor approximates natural convection.</li>
<li><b>ε = 0.95; fr = 0.73; σ = 5.670374419 × 10⁻⁸ W/(m² K⁴); LR = 16.5 K/kPa.</b> Radiation and vapour-resistance formulation are based on <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6397408/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cramer &amp; Jay, Partitional calorimetry (2019)</a>. fr is an approximate standing radiating-area fraction. Re is in m² kPa/W, so A Δp/Re is W.</li>
<li><b>Saturation pressure:</b> the <a href="https://www.fao.org/4/x0490e/x0490e07.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FAO Tetens relationship</a>, with T in °C and pressure in kPa.</li>
<li><b>M = 115, 230, 400 or 600 W:</b> deliberately rounded teaching examples for rest through heavy activity, informed by the scale of metabolic workloads in <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/publications/numbered/2016-106.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIOSH (2016)</a>. They are assigned net heat production, after external work, not measured rates or universal activity definitions.</li></ul>
<p class="fine">The screen rounds to whole watts; calculations retain full precision. Independently rounded terms can differ by 1–2 W when added. “&lt;1 W” identifies small nonzero flows. Numerical balance uses a 10⁻⁸ W tolerance, not a physiological comfort threshold.</p>
<h3>Six agreed classroom scenarios · A–F</h3><p>Eight rows represent the six scenarios: E compares humidity and F compares air movement. These are equation and teaching-behaviour checks, not validation against human experiments. Dry values below are positive losses and negative gains; K = 0 W in all cases.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Whole-body heat rates (W). Inputs: °C / RH % / m/s / activity.</caption><thead><tr><th>Scenario / inputs</th><th>M</th><th>Cconv</th><th>R</th><th>Ereq</th><th>Emax</th><th>E</th><th>S</th></tr></thead><tbody id="benchmark-rows"></tbody></table></div>
<p id="validation-summary" class="fine"></p>
<p>At 33°C, convection and radiation vanish. Above it, both reverse. In hot dry air, wind increases both convective heat gain and evaporative capacity. In air with no evaporative capacity, extra wind increases heat gain instead. A small positive S is not rounded into a claim of physiological safety.</p>
<h3>Known limits</h3><p>All outputs are quantitatively approximate. This is a hybrid steady transport model, not a validated individual thermoregulation model. Fixed skin temperature becomes especially unrealistic in cold air. Full skin wettedness and unlimited sweat supply make capacity-based balance optimistic. Omitted respiratory heat loss and skin diffusion affect the budget, particularly during exercise and at rest. No sweat secretion rate, core temperature, exposure time, comfort score or clinical outcome is predicted.</p>
<h3>Future versions — deliberately deferred</h3><ul><li><b>V2:</b> clothing, solar/radiant load, air versus water.</li><li><b>V3:</b> skin blood flow, dynamic skin temperature, sweating control and shivering.</li><li><b>V4:</b> hydration, sweat-rate limits and cardiovascular strain.</li><li><b>V5:</b> acclimatization: sweating thresholds, sweat composition and plasma volume.</li><li><b>V6:</b> body size, surface-area-to-mass ratio and integrated hot/cold environments.</li></ul><p class="fine">The model keeps dry exchange, evaporative capacity, heat production and rendering separate so these extensions can be added deliberately. The interactive calculation uses no external scripts or fonts and stores no student data. Scene images are illustrative; they add no environmental variables. Source links are optional reading.</p>
